  "description": "Compute forward pass of residual building block with two linear-ReLU stages and additive identity or projection shortcut.\n\nDeep non-residual architectures suffer vanishing gradients during backpropagation. Identity shortcut mappings enable gradients to propagate directly to early layers via additive identity paths.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\mathbf{y} = \\text{ReLU}\\left(\\mathcal{F}(\\mathbf{x}) + \\mathbf{x}_{\\text{skip}}\\right), \\quad \\mathcal{F}(\\mathbf{x}) = \\text{ReLU}(\\mathbf{x} \\mathbf{W}_1 + \\mathbf{b}_1) \\mathbf{W}_2 + \\mathbf{b}_2\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Compute first linear layer x W1 + b1 followed by ReLU activation\n2. Project through second linear layer h1 W2 + b2\n3. Add identity shortcut x (or linear projection x Ws if provided) and apply final ReLU\n\n### Returns\nNested 2D list representing activated block output rounded to 4 decimal places.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: ResNet Residual Block\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNeural networks achieve their remarkable expressive power by stacking linear layers interspersed with non-linear activation functions and normalization layers. Without non-linear activations, any deep network would collapse into a single shallow linear regression. **ResNet Residual Block** ensures signals propagate cleanly through dozens or hundreds of layers without vanishing or exploding.\n\nDeep non-residual architectures suffer vanishing gradients during backpropagation. Identity shortcut mappings enable gradients to propagate directly to early layers via additive identity paths. In this problem, the goal is to: compute forward pass of residual building block with two linear-relu stages and additive identity or projection shortcut.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y} = \\text{ReLU}\\left(\\mathcal{F}(\\mathbf{x}) + \\mathbf{x}_{\\text{skip}}\\right), \\quad \\mathcal{F}(\\mathbf{x}) = \\text{ReLU}(\\mathbf{x} \\mathbf{W}_1 + \\mathbf{b}_1) \\mathbf{W}_2 + \\mathbf{b}_2\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Compute first linear layer x W1 + b1 followed by ReLU activation\n2. **Phase 2**: Project through second linear layer h1 W2 + b2\n3. **Phase 3**: Add identity shortcut x (or linear projection x Ws if provided) and apply final ReLU\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Gradient Flow**: Carefully designed activation functions (like ReLU, GELU, and Swish) maintain strong gradient signals during backpropagation.\n* **Internal Covariate Shift**: Normalization techniques (like LayerNorm and RMSNorm) standardize hidden states across channels or batch dimensions, allowing models to train stably at higher learning rates.\n* **Hardware Friendly**: Modern deep learning frameworks implement these layers using fused GPU kernels to reduce memory round-trips.",
